# Runtime

`_KDB.runtime.<owner>` holds per-session technical objects of one owner: live DOM and jQuery refs, listener records, bus unsubscribe handles, AbortControllers, observers, timer ids, pending promises, Cognos facet stamps, transient drag mechanics. It is never serialised, persisted, written back or restored. A value that must survive the session belongs in `_KDB.state` with a canonical writer.

## Standard buckets

Use these names; do not invent synonyms.

| Bucket | Holds |
|---|---|
| `abortControllers` | AbortControllers (preferred cleanup mechanism) |
| `listeners` | DOM listener records when `{ signal }` cannot be used |
| `subscriptions` | `_KDB.events` unsubscribe functions when `{ signal }` is not used |
| `observers` | MutationObserver, ResizeObserver, IntersectionObserver |
| `timers` | timeout, interval and rAF ids as `{ kind, id }` (kit `scheduleTimeout` / `scheduleFrame`); nested id maps are named `<thing>By<Key>` (`watchdogsByMountKey`) |
| `domRefs` | DOM nodes the module mounted or resolved |
| `pendingPromises` | in-flight work; cleanup aborts it and ignores late results |
| `adapters`, `customControls` | live instances created at mount |

Use the kit (`core/utils/kit/runtime.js`) instead of per-module `ensure*Runtime` / `cleanup*Runtime` files: `ensureRuntime(_KDB, owner)` gets or creates the owner object and its buckets without replacing it (facet stamps and owner fields survive); `disposeRuntimeBuckets(rt)` releases recognised entries and resets the buckets, and returns errors instead of throwing; `addListener(rt.listeners, key, target, type, handler)` stores a canonical listener record. Store only shapes the kit recognises (release functions, listener records, `{ unsubscribe }`, AbortControllers, observers, timer entries); anything else is skipped silently. Never assign `_KDB.runtime.<owner> = {...}` wholesale.

## Cleanup

- Every listener, subscription, observer, timer and controller has a cleanup path that runs in `destroy` (and on re-render for per-render resources). "Clean up later" is not a path.
- Prefer one AbortController per scope (module mount, popup open, drag session):

```js
var scope = new AbortController();
_KDB.runtime.kdbControl.abortControllers.panel = scope;
document.addEventListener("mousedown", onOutsideClick, { signal: scope.signal });
_KDB.events.subscribe(_tabsEvents.CHANGED, onTabsChanged, { signal: scope.signal });
// cleanup
scope.abort();
```

- `_KDB.events.subscribe` accepts `{ signal }` and also returns an unsubscribe function.
- Replacing a resource (re-open, re-bind) aborts or clears the old one first.
- Widget-scoped resources are cleared when the widget is removed, before its lifecycle changes.
- Bootstrap resources in `_KDB.runtime.core` belong to `KDB.js`.
- Waits use timeouts and the signal (kit `sleep`, `pollUntil`); abort listeners are removed when the wait ends.

## Owner-private

Other modules do not read or write `_KDB.runtime.<owner>`. They use the owner's API, selectors or events. Known existing violations (`runtime.cognosHost.chromeChecks`, `runtime.tabs.pendingPromises`, `runtime.<owner>.historyReplayHandlerContribution`, `runtime.history.writebackRestore`, `runtime.core.domRefs`) are scheduled to become real APIs in Phase 2. Do not add new ones.

DOM refs are valid for one render cycle; after a rebuild, resolve them again from selectors.
